PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The question requires identifying the *least* plausible explanation among the eight, using historical context around the 1676 Bansenshukai (a concrete Iga-ryū compilation by Fujibayashi Yasutake, later copied repeatedly in the Edo period) and the consistent pattern of the same blank circles across every surviving manuscript.  

H is the weakest by far. It fabricates an ahistorical link between the Kuji-in hand seals, “nine holes” of the female body, Taoist erotic rituals, and yin-yang energy work that the Bansenshukai itself never describes. No extant copy, secondary source, or contemporary record supports such a reading; the text’s actual kunoichi passages concern disguise, intelligence-gathering, and social infiltration, not sexual mysticism. The remaining options, while varying in probability, at least rest on documented practices (redaction of sensitive material, physical wear of heavily consulted sections, moral scruples of Confucian-era copyists, or oral-mnemonic coding). H alone is pure retrofitted fantasy and therefore the least plausible.}

